A descriptor backup goes only where a backup belongs

write_bgroup_block wrote the group-descriptor table into every block
group. It consulted has_superblock() only to work out the offset, never
to decide whether to write at all -- so on a volume with sparse_super,
which is every filesystem this builds, fifty-five of sixty-four copies
landed where no reader looks for one.

They were not free. Each is a 4 KiB write at a different group's start,
128 MB from its neighbour, and a USB stick charges an erase cycle for a
scattered write however little it carries. After the inode tables stopped
being zeroed, writes like these were what a format's time consisted of:
122 of 128 writes were 4 KiB or smaller and came to 575 KB between them,
and the format took ninety seconds on a DataTraveler.

73 writes now, from 128 -- and from 372 before any of this.

Measured and checked: the geometry sweep is clean on all 117 combinations
of size, block size and generation; write 114, differential 36, newfs 16,
bounds 23; and the Linux kernel mounts the result, writes 64 MB into it,
leaves e2fsck clean and logs nothing.

One thing this does not fix, recorded because it was found on the way and
is real. A group marked BLOCK_UNINIT has its bitmap derived rather than
read, and the bitmap this mkfs leaves on disk marks only the padding, not
the group's own metadata. The two readers therefore disagree if anything
ever reads instead of deriving -- which e2fsck does when recovering from a
backup superblock, where it reports block-bitmap differences across every
group and repairs them. mke2fs avoids this by writing complete bitmaps.
Writing padding-only ones here was tried and changed nothing; generating
complete ones is the actual fix and is not attempted here.
